<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mozaffar ad-Din Shah Qajar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1853">1853</a>–<a href="/years/1907">1907</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Mozaffar ad-Din Shah · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mozaffar ad-Din Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> was shah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> from 1896 to 1907, succeeding his assassinated father Naser al-Din Shah. Short of money, he took large Russian loans and granted concessions, including the D'Arcy oil concession of 1901. Facing the Constitutional Revolution, he signed the constitution of December 1906 that created an elected parliament, the Majlis, shortly before his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mozaffar-ad-din-shah-qajar.jpg" alt="Mozaffar ad-Din Shah Qajar" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Golestan Palace · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mozaffar_ad-Din_Shah_Qajar_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mozaffar ad-Din was born on 23 March 1853 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, the fourth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naser-al-din-shah-qajar">Naser al-Din Shah Qajar</a>. His mother was Shokouh al-Saltaneh, a daughter of Fathollah Mirza and a granddaughter of the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> shah, Fath-Ali Shah. He was named crown prince and sent as governor to the northern province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> in 1861.</p><p>He spent some 35 years as crown prince, in the pursuit of pleasure. His relations with his father were frequently strained, and he was not consulted in important matters of state, so that when he came to the throne in May 1896 he was unprepared for the burdens of office.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> faced a financial crisis at his accession, with annual government expenditure far above revenue. Mozaffar ad-Din attempted some reforms of the central treasury, but the debts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> incurred by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> court undermined them. He borrowed further from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Russia, and the later loans went to pay off earlier ones rather than to fund economic development. To meet the costs of the state and of his own extravagant life he also signed concessions that gave foreigners monopoly control over various industries and markets.</p><p>Like his father he visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> three times. On the urging of his chancellor Amin-os-Soltan he borrowed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> to pay for the journeys. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> he saw the cinematographe and ordered his photographer to bring the equipment to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, which began Persian cinema. On his visit to the United Kingdom in August 1902 he hoped for the Order of the Garter, but King Edward VII refused it to a non-Christian; in 1903 the king gave way and the shah was appointed a member.</p><p>Fear among the aristocracy, the educated elite and the religious leaders of foreign control led to protests in 1906. The shah then agreed to the creation of a Majles, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-consultative-assembly">National Consultative Assembly</a>, granting a constitution and a parliament that curtailed the monarch's power. He died of a heart attack 40 days after granting the constitution, on 8 January 1907, and was buried in the Imam Husayn Shrine in Kerbala.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Contemporaries judged his character in varied terms. The historian Abbas Amanat describes a man of gentle disposition with an earnest wish to open the country to social and educational reforms, who lacked his father's panache and political skill in playing the European powers against each other. The British diplomat Mortimer Durand, who knew both shahs, wrote that he was more amiable than his father but weak and easily misled. He suffered ill health from youth, including a weak heart and a chronic kidney infection, yet enjoyed riding, hunting and shooting. He is often credited with the Persian Constitution of 1906.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mozaffar ad-Din Shah Qajar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Constitutional Revolution transformed the system: in 1906 <b>Mozaffar ad-Din Shah</b> granted a constitution and an elected parliament, the Majlis, and the Supplementary Fundamental Law of 1907 established a constitutional monarchy with guarantees of rights, although the clergy were given a role in reviewing legislation for its conformity…</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1905 protests arose over tariffs imposed to repay the Russian loan that had financed <b>Mozaffar ad-Din Shah</b>'s tour of Europe.</blockquote><a href="/movement/persian-constitutional-revolution">Persian Constitutional Revolution · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.69,51.39&amp;year=1888" aria-label="Tehran on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Mozaffar ad-Din Shah Qajar"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4404 4492h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4404 4492h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.69,51.39&amp;year=1888">What was here in 1888? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
